    "case_id": "39",
    "case_summary": "A 47-year-old woman comes to the physician for a follow-up examination. She has noticed trembling of her hands for the past 5 months. These movements are only present when she reaches to pick up an object or answer the phone. She has a history of major depressive disorder but has been off medications for the past 3 years. Her father had Parkinson's disease and died 6 months ago. She drinks a glass of wine daily. She appears anxious. Her temperature is 37 °C (98.6 °F), pulse is 80/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. A low-frequency tremor is present that worsens as she reaches out for an object. She consistently overshoots her target. There is no tremor present when her arms are held outstretched. There is increased tone in the lower extremities and Babinski's sign is positive bilaterally. What is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Parkinson's disease\nB. Essential tremor\nC. Multiple sclerosis\nD. Cerebrovascular disease\nE. Cerebellar demyelination\nF. None\nG. None\n",
